                <pb facs="0395" n="137" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg137"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/1571</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-03-10T17:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March, 10, 1915, 5 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 8 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1762. Your 1182, February 24.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d190-f1"/> Much inquiry has
                    failed to show that any order has been issued by the British Government to
                    merchant vessels to arm themselves; but there is reason to believe that the
                    Government has permitted arming when ships have asked. There is no evidence that
                    I have yet found, however, that any transatlantic British merchantmen have been
                    armed. And all the arming that may have been done has been done since the German
                    submarine “blockade” began and it has been done with reference to German
                    submarine attacks.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
